  "estimatedMinutes": null,
  "statement": "Let \\(f(x)=1+\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{x^{2}}{4}+\\frac{x^{3}}{8}+\\ldots\\), for \\(-1 \\leq x \\leq 1\\). Find \\(\\sqrt{e^{\\int_{0}^{1} f(x) d x}}\\).",
  "answerChoices": [],
  "answer": {
    "letter": null,
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.hmmt-feb-calculus-1998-p4.hmmt",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Observe that \\(f(x)\\) is merely an infinite geometric series. Thus \\(f(x)=\\frac{1}{1-\\frac{x}{2}}=\\frac{2}{2-x}\\). Then \\(\\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{2}{2-x}=2 \\ln 2\\). Then \\(\\sqrt{e^{2 \\ln 2}}=\\sqrt{2^{2}}=2\\).",
